    en: {
      title: "How to read a campaign report in 10 minutes",
      desc: "Quick steps to read any campaign report and find where money leaks, instead of drowning in numbers.",
      tldr: "Start from the bottom line (profit) and work backwards: ROAS, cost, conversion, then the ad itself.",
      body: [
        {"p": "Campaign reports come full of numbers, and the problem is not their quantity but their order. Read top to bottom and you get lost in impressions and clicks before reaching the real question: is this campaign earning or losing me money?"},
        {"h": "Step 1: start from the outcome"},
        {"ul": ["Actual revenue from your store or CRM, not only the platform number.", "Ad spend over the same period.", "ROAS compared with your product's break-even."]},
        {"h": "Step 2: find the biggest leak"},
        {"p": "Compare the journey stages: impression, click, visit, add to cart, payment. The biggest drop between two stages is the first thing to fix. If people click and add to cart but do not pay, the problem is usually shipping, payment options or trust, not the ad."},
        {"h": "Step 3: separate the causes"},
        {"ul": ["Cost per click rose: usually competition or creative fatigue.", "Conversion rate fell: check the page, the offer and the price.", "Returns are high: check the product, the order source and confirmation before shipping."]},
        {"h": "Step 4: one decision"},
        {"p": "End every review with one clear decision: scale a specific campaign, stop an ad, or test a new offer. A report without a decision is just numbers."},
        {"p": "If you want someone to read your campaigns with you and come out with decisions, book a free consultation and send your numbers."},
      ],
    },
